00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:29,659
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

118
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:40,260
There are many ways to build into our scouting program

119
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:46,520
That part of the Scout Oath in which we pledge our duty to God, and the 12th point of the Scout Law: a scout is reverent.

120
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:55,340
Perhaps it's best to know that there are some policy statements that kind of help us shape and identify what those things are.

121
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:57,680
Let me run through them with you.

122
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:07,220
The activities of the members of the Boy Scouts of America shall be carried on in conditions which show respect to the convictions of others in matter of custom and religion,

123
00:08:07,460 --> 00:08:11,380
as required by the 12th point of the Scout Law, reading reverent.

124
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:16,180
A Scout's reverent towards God, he is faithful in his religious duties and he respects the beliefs of others.

125
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:25,620
In no case where a unit is connected with a church or other distinctively religious organizations shall members of other denominations or faith be required,

126
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:33,799
because of their membership in the unit, to take part in or observe a religious ceremony distinctly unique to that organization or church.

127
00:08:33,799 --> 00:08:43,260
The Boy Scouts of America recognizes the religious element in the training of the member but is absolutely non-sectarian in its attitudes towards that religious training.

128
00:08:44,179 --> 00:08:52,859
Its policy is that the home and the organization or group with which the member is connected shall give definite attention to religious life.

129
00:08:53,620 --> 00:08:56,219
So what do we take away from these things?

130
00:08:56,220 --> 00:09:07,740
Well, most of the places that I've experienced scouting, the scouts and leaders, as is the general demographic in the United States, identify themselves as Christians.

131
00:09:08,280 --> 00:09:20,540
It's more common for us to see a non-denominational Christian observance in our troops and our camps than it is to see a non-sectarian or inter-faith observance.

132
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:21,900
What's the difference?

133
00:09:21,900 --> 00:09:40,020
A non-denominational Christian observance is based on the assumption that all present identify themselves as Christians, although they may be Catholic, they may be Protestant and they may be one of the many flavors of Protestantism.

134
00:09:40,819 --> 00:09:47,260
They might be Methodists or Presbyterians or Baptists or whatever.

135
00:09:47,260 --> 00:09:54,700
Yet all of the members of those different denominations share a few common touch points in Christianity.

136
00:09:56,060 --> 00:10:06,100
A non-sectarian, inter-faith service understands that not all the members present identify themselves as Christian.

137
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:14,340
There may be Buddhists, there may be Muslims, Hindus, there may be members of many different faiths present.

138
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:19,240
So the service needs to be conducted with a sensitivity to that fact.

139
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:26,040
For example, in our troop right now, while the vast majority of the scouts and leaders would identify themselves as being Christian,

140
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:30,820
we also have ones that would identify themselves as Muslim and as Buddhist,

141
00:10:31,420 --> 00:10:34,560
And I have had Jewish families participate in the troop in the past,

142
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:46,300
so we go to great lengths to make sure that our observations of our religious duty are designed so that all who are members of our troop can participate.

143
00:10:46,939 --> 00:10:52,619
What we generally do is every weekend that we're camping is we have a brief Vespers service.

144
00:10:53,180 --> 00:11:00,500
Vespers is Latin for evening and it's a very familiar term, I think, to many Christian denominations who have evening services,

145
00:11:00,500 --> 00:11:09,700
And we use the word Vespers very advisedly because it indicates that this is something special that is set aside for the observation of our duty to God.

146
00:11:09,700 --> 00:11:12,560
and the twelfth point of the Scout Law, that is, reverence.

147
00:11:13,080 --> 00:11:17,400
Vespers opens once the troop assembles with a short reflection.

148
00:11:18,840 --> 00:11:25,220
It might be something very simple. like you know, today on our hike, I watched as you followed the trailblazes along the way.

149
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:31,840
These blazes help us follow the trail, alert us to changes ahead, like turns and road crossings,

150
00:11:32,340 --> 00:11:37,500
And I couldn't help but think that, while they might not be painted on trees like trailblazes,

151
00:11:37,680 --> 00:11:41,960
there are signs and blazes that we follow as we live our lives.

152
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:45,940
The Scout Oath and Law is kind of like a set of blazes that guide us,

153
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:49,100
as are your families, your friends and your beliefs.

154
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:53,400
Can any of you tell me a trailblaze that you follow?

155
00:11:53,400 --> 00:12:00,380
Now, that's an example of a very open-ended, brief reflection that encourages the Scouts to participate,

156
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:07,180
And then what follows is: is any of the Scouts and leaders can add to that reflection as they see fit,

157
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:09,939
focusing on the exchange of ideas,

158
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:17,840
And then, once everyone who desires to has spoken, we'll go ahead and close with a moment of silence.

159
00:12:17,840 --> 00:12:22,920
The moment of silence is a pretty familiar thing to most of us,

160
00:12:23,500 --> 00:12:32,200
And it consciences all faiths, because some faiths don't pray in the same way that Christians pray.

161
00:12:32,780 --> 00:12:35,160
For instance, Buddhists don't say prayers.

162
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:37,240
So that gives us a moment,

163
00:12:37,360 --> 00:12:40,620
And then the Vespers' observance is concluded.

164
00:12:40,620 --> 00:12:43,300
It's very brief, usually about 15 or 20 minutes,

165
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:49,620
And it doesn't task everyone's powers of concentration.

166
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:54,320
It's held, like I said, usually of a Saturday evening,

167
00:12:55,100 --> 00:13:02,960
And so it is a brief, succinct observance that serves as a reminder of our duty to God and our pledge to be ready.

